{"name":"Gold Thread","hex":"#fee8b0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold thread","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A luxurious textile or embroidery thread made by interlacing or wrapping a core of fine silk yarn with thin strips or wires of precious metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold_thread"}],"entries":[{"title":"Goldwork (embroidery)","blocks":[{"text":"Goldwork is the art of embroidery using metal threads. It is particularly prized for the way light plays on it. The term \"goldwork\" is used even when the threads are imitation gold, silver, or copper. The metal wires used to make the threads have never been entirely gold; they have always been gold-coated silver or cheaper metals, and even then the \"gold\" often contains a very low percentage of real gold. Most metal threads are available in silver and sometimes copper as well as gold; some are available in colors as well."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Skirt_%28AM_1944.51-1%29.jpg/330px-Skirt_%28AM_1944.51-1%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldwork_(embroidery)","wikidata":"Q1304791"},{"title":"Coptis","blocks":[{"text":"Coptis is a genus of between 10 and 15 species of flowering plants in the family Ranunculaceae, native to Asia and North America."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Coptis_occidentalis.jpg/330px-Coptis_occidentalis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coptis","wikidata":"Q135562"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/WLA_ima_mans_formal_court_robe_chaopao.jpg/500px-WLA_ima_mans_formal_court_robe_chaopao.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/WLA_ima_mans_formal_court_robe_chaopao.jpg/1280px-WLA_ima_mans_formal_court_robe_chaopao.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/WLA_ima_mans_formal_court_robe_chaopao.jpg/500px-WLA_ima_mans_formal_court_robe_chaopao.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A style of Mangfu, used as court dress in the Qing dynasty, embroidered with gold and silk metallic thread, late 1700s","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWLA_ima_mans_formal_court_robe_chaopao.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/The_7th_Marquess_of_Cholmondeley_Allan_Warren_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-The_7th_Marquess_of_Cholmondeley_Allan_Warren_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm